+``subtitle-layout [<track>]``
+    Return the latest retained structured layout for ``primary`` subtitles.
+    ``track`` defaults to ``primary`` and no other value is supported. This
+    command is available even when the feature is disabled or was not compiled
+    in, so clients can inspect ``status`` and ``capabilities``.
+
+    The result always contains ``capabilities``, effective ``limits``,
+    ``available``, ``status``, and ``revision``. Builds without compatible
+    layout support return an empty capability list and limits map.
+    ``available`` is true exactly when ``status`` is ``ok`` and the response
+    also contains a snapshot. Other
+    status values are ``disabled``, ``unsupported-build``,
+    ``unsupported-format``, ``unsupported-render-mode``, ``empty``,
+    ``no-authoritative-render``, ``limit-exceeded``, ``collection-failed``, and
+    ``stale-identity``. An empty render is not a usable snapshot.
+
+    ``limits`` reports the effective aggregate bounds for events, parsed text
+    bytes, layout units, bitmap pixels, and retained snapshot bytes. Exceeding one reports
+    ``limit-exceeded`` without affecting ordinary subtitle rendering.
+
+    A successful result additionally contains:
+
+    ``snapshot_id``
+        Opaque identity for the immutable response.
+
+    ``media`` and ``track``
+        The playing playlist entry, primary track and decoder generation that
+        produced the snapshot. ``track`` also reports the source ``format``,
+        ``layout_engine`` and ``layout_unit_mode``. The mode is
+        ``harfbuzz-cluster`` for complex shaping and ``simple-scalar`` for
+        libass's simple shaper.
+
+    ``time``
+        Video and subtitle timestamps from the render which acquired this
+        layout. They remain part of the immutable snapshot until its semantic
+        contents change. Event start and end times use the subtitle clock after
+        mpv subtitle timing transforms. A null event end means that its
+        duration is unknown.
+
+    ``space``
+        The OSD width, height, margins and display pixel aspect ratio accepted
+        by the render, plus ``render_origin=output-render``. Rectangles are
+        half-open and use the same coordinates as ``osd-dimensions`` and
+        ``mouse-pos``. An embedding application must forward pointer positions
+        in this coordinate space or perform its own surface-to-OSD mapping.
+
+    ``events``
+        Active events in renderer order. Each event contains a snapshot-local
+        ID, order, timing, parsed logical ``text``, ``text_index_unit=utf8-byte``,
+        ``units``, and the final whole-event bitmap bounds when available.
+        Unit ranges are contiguous byte ranges over that event's text. The
+        range unit depends on ``layout_unit_mode`` and is not promised to be a
+        grapheme, word, or code point in complex shaping mode.
+
+        ``logical_rects`` are pre-transform typographic boxes after line layout
+        and collision placement. They do not represent final per-unit visible pixels.
+
+        The ``event-geometry-profile`` capability adds nullable ``geometry_profile``.
+        ``static-logical-v2`` identifies a restricted source grammar checked by
+        mpv against the exact rendered libass event: plain text, one static
+        ``pos`` tag, positive decimal ``fscx``/``fscy`` tags, decimal karaoke
+        timing tags (``k``, ``K``, ``kf``, ``ko``), and hexadecimal color/alpha
+        tags. Color values allow up to eight digits and an optional trailing
+        ampersand; alpha values allow up to two digits. Unknown or malformed
+        tags, resets, effects, rotation, vertical fonts and nonpositive style
+        scales are excluded. Admission requires requested libass pixel aspect 1 and
+        no ASS style override for authored ASS. Converted SubRip uses mpv's
+        generated style. Rectangles already include OSD margins.
+        Null means the event or rendering options are outside this profile,
+        or the runtime lacks the event index needed to verify them.
+
+        The ``event-track-index`` capability adds ``track_index``, the event's
+        index in the exact rendered libass track. Sorting by this index follows
+        the traversal used by mpv's subtitle text properties. It is scoped to
+        the retained snapshot, not a persistent source-file identity. Filtered,
+        empty or unrendered events can be absent; clients must verify their
+        complete active-event binding rather than assuming positional equality.
+        ``sub-text/ass-full`` serializes times at centisecond precision while
+        event timing in the layout retains millisecond precision.
+
+        This profile describes logical geometry, not final pixel visibility,
+        paint eligibility or a lease on future layout. Karaoke colors and alpha
+        may change or hide text without changing its semantic regions. All
+        snapshot validation and invalidation rules still apply. No outlines or
+        additional libass output flags are required.
+
+    The snapshot is replaced atomically. Repeated fetches return identical data
+    while the current ``snapshot_id`` and revision remain unchanged. Observe
+    ``subtitle-layout-revision`` and fetch after it changes; an
+    observation can be coalesced, so clients must treat the command as a latest
+    state snapshot rather than a lossless event stream. The command constructs
+    and serializes the bounded response synchronously, which can be expensive
+    near the reported limits. Do not poll it when the revision is unchanged.
+
+    JSON IPC example::
+
+        {"command":["subtitle-layout","primary"]}
+
+    A named libmpv command node or Lua command uses ``_name``::
+
+        mp.command_native({_name="subtitle-layout",
+                           track="primary"})
+
+``subtitle-layout-valid [<snapshot_id>]``
+    Return whether ``snapshot_id`` identifies the current retained snapshot for
+    the currently playing file, primary track, and decoder generation. This is
+    a point-in-time check: player state can change after the command returns.
+    Consumers should discard installed geometry whenever the observed revision
+    changes. An omitted or unknown ID returns false.
+
 Input and Keybind Commands
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
